* ============================================================================
* ESTIMATED gate capacitance for igzo_mmm_lab - NOT MEASURED
* ============================================================================
*
* WHAT THIS IS FOR.  The measured PDK knows one capacitance: Cox, from the
* 400 x 400 um plate.  It knows nothing about the SEMICONDUCTOR's own
* capacitance, because no C-V sweep has been taken on a transistor.  Every AC
* number this laboratory has produced therefore rests on the oxide alone.
*
* This file supplies an estimate, so that a circuit can be simulated with a
* plausible gate capacitance before the measurement exists, and so that the
* measurement - when it is taken - has something to be compared against.
*
* NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS MEASURED ON THIS PROCESS.  Every number is either
* geometry this PDK already knows, or a value from the literature on
* amorphous IGZO.  Do not quote a result that depends on it without saying
* which of the two it came from.
*
* HOW TO USE IT
*
*     .include $PDK_ROOT/$PDK/libs.tech/ngspice/design.ngspice
*     .include $PDK_ROOT/$PDK/libs.tech/ngspice/igzo_cv_estimated.ngspice
*     .lib     $PDK_ROOT/$PDK/libs.tech/ngspice/igzo_mmm_lab.ngspice best
*     XM1 d g s igzo_tft_cv W=1000u L=8u ov=5u
*
* `igzo_tft_cv` is `igzo_tft` with the estimated capacitance added.  The
* plain `igzo_tft` is untouched, so nothing that exists today changes
* behaviour by including this file.
*
* WHEN THE MEASUREMENT ARRIVES: replace the three .param values below with
* the extracted ones and delete the ESTIMATED warnings.  Nothing else has to
* change - not the wrapper, not the testbench, not a netlist.
*
* ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE PHYSICS, AND WHERE EACH NUMBER COMES FROM
*
* A top-gate TFT's gate sees the oxide in series with whatever the
* semiconductor presents:
*
*     1/C_gate = 1/C_ox + 1/C_semiconductor
*
* In strong accumulation the accumulation layer sits within a nanometre or
* two of the interface, C_semiconductor is very large, and C_gate -> C_ox.
* With the channel depleted, the whole 30 nm film is the dielectric and
* C_semiconductor is at its minimum.  The estimate covers both ends.
*
*   eps_igzo = 16          relative permittivity of amorphous IGZO.
*                          [1] Chasin et al., "Flexible indium-gallium-zinc-
*                          oxide Schottky diode operating beyond 2.45 GHz",
*                          Nature Communications 6, 7561 (2015), which
*                          extracts eps_r = 16 from its C-V.  Values from 10
*                          to 16 appear across the literature; 16 is the
*                          pessimistic end for AC loading, which is the end
*                          worth designing against.
*
*   t_igzo = 30 nm         NOT from the literature - this is the film
*                          thickness this PDK already states, in
*                          libs.tech/magic/igzo_mmm_lab.tech ("Film
*                          thicknesses: 30 nm IGZO").
*
*   n_igzo = 1e16 cm^-3    free carrier concentration of the undoped film.
*                          [2] Facing-target-sputtered a-IGZO measured at
*                          3.6e15 to 7.4e15 cm^-3 by Hall; plasma-treated
*                          films reach 1e19 and above.  1e16 is a round
*                          number inside the undoped range.  It is used ONLY
*                          to check that the film fully depletes - see below -
*                          and no simulated current depends on it.
*
* THE CHECK THAT MAKES THIS USABLE.  The Debye length in the film is
*
*     L_D = sqrt(eps_igzo * kT / (q^2 * n))  =  48 nm at n = 1e16 cm^-3
*
* which is longer than the 30 nm film.  The channel film therefore depletes
* all the way through, so C_semiconductor bottoms out at eps_igzo/t_igzo
* rather than at some bias-dependent depletion width.  That is what makes a
* two-capacitor series a defensible estimate instead of a hand-wave, and it
* is why n appears in this file at all.
*
*   C_ox     = 1.564 fF/um^2   MEASURED, from design.ngspice
*   C_igzo   = 4.72 fF/um^2    estimated: eps0*16/30nm
*   C_series = 1.174 fF/um^2   the two in series - 75 % of the oxide alone
*
* So the estimate says: a fully depleted channel presents about three
* quarters of the capacitance the oxide alone would suggest, and an
* accumulated one presents very nearly all of it.  If a C-V measurement comes
* back flat at Cox, the semiconductor is more conductive than assumed; if it
* comes back near 1.17 fF/um^2 and stays there, it is not accumulating.
* Either outcome is informative, which is the point of writing the estimate
* down before measuring.
* ============================================================================

.param
+  eps0        = 8.8541878e-12   $ F/m     vacuum permittivity
+  eps_igzo    = 16              $ -       ESTIMATED, literature [1]
+  t_igzo      = 30e-9           $ m       this PDK's own stated thickness
+  n_igzo      = 1e22            $ m^-3    ESTIMATED, literature [2]
*  Reference values at the default thickness, for reading and for hand
*  checks.  The wrapper recomputes them from its own t_igzo so that the
*  instance parameter actually does something.
+  c_igzo_area = 'eps0*eps_igzo/t_igzo'
+  c_series_area = '1/(1/cox_area + 1/c_igzo_area)'
*  Where the capacitance turns over, and how sharply.  vth_cv is the
*  best-corner threshold; vt_cv is a SHAPE PARAMETER with no measurement
*  behind it - it is how wide the transition is in volts, and a C-V sweep is
*  exactly the measurement that would replace it.
+  vth_cv      = 0.09            $ V       best-corner Vto
+  vt_cv       = 0.6             $ V       ESTIMATED transition width

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* igzo_tft_cv - the device, with the estimated gate capacitance
*
* Identical to igzo_tft except that the gate capacitances see the oxide and
* the semiconductor in series rather than the oxide alone, and a channel
* capacitance is added that the level-1 core does not provide.
*
* `acc` selects the regime, because level 1 cannot switch between them by
* itself:
*     acc = 0   channel depleted - the pessimistic AC case, and the default
*     acc = 1   channel accumulated - capacitance tends to the oxide value
* A real device moves between the two with VGS.  Sweeping `acc` is how this
* estimate brackets that, and it is deliberately a knob rather than a hidden
* formula: a smooth transition here would look like knowledge nobody has.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.subckt igzo_tft_cv d g s W=100u L=10u ov=5u nf=1 B=0 b_scale=1 acc=0
+  t_igzo=30e-9 vdep=1
* The series is formed HERE, from the instance's own t_igzo, not from the
* global .param above: a global would make every instance share one thickness
* and the parameter would be silently ignored - which is exactly what
* happened on the first attempt.
.param c_igzo_inst = 'eps0*eps_igzo/t_igzo'
.param c_series_inst = '1/(1/cox_area + 1/c_igzo_inst)'
.param mr_cv = '1 + (mu_corner * b_scale * B) * (mu_corner * b_scale * B)'
Rd  d  di 'rc_w/W'
Rs  s  si 'rc_w/W'
M1  di g  si si IGZO_TFT W='W/mr_cv' L='L'
*
* THE GATE CAPACITANCE, AND HOW IT MOVES WITH BIAS
*
* Between the two limits - oxide alone when the channel is accumulated,
* oxide in series with the film when it is depleted - a real device moves
* smoothly, and where it sits is set by the gate voltage.  The weight `fa`
* below carries that:
*
*     fa = 0.5 * (1 + tanh((Vgs - vth_cv) / vt_cv))
*
* which is 0 well below threshold, 1 well above, and passes through 0.5 at
* vth_cv.  It is a SHAPE, not a derivation: nothing in the measurements says
* how sharp the transition is, and vt_cv is the knob that admits it.  A
* surface-potential model would produce this curve from physics; level 1
* cannot, and pretending otherwise would be worse than saying so.
*
*   vdep = 1  (default)  capacitance follows Vgs through fa
*   vdep = 0             capacitance is pinned by `acc`, 0 = depleted,
*                        1 = accumulated.  This is what the bracketing
*                        testbench uses, and what the check asserts against.
Cgd g  di C='ov*W*(cox_area*(vdep*(0.5*(1+tanh((v(g,si)-vth_cv)/vt_cv)))
+     + (1-vdep)*acc) + c_series_inst*(1-(vdep*(0.5*(1+tanh((v(g,si)-vth_cv)
+     /vt_cv))) + (1-vdep)*acc)))'
Cgs g  si C='ov*W*(cox_area*(vdep*(0.5*(1+tanh((v(g,si)-vth_cv)/vt_cv)))
+     + (1-vdep)*acc) + c_series_inst*(1-(vdep*(0.5*(1+tanh((v(g,si)-vth_cv)
+     /vt_cv))) + (1-vdep)*acc)))'
.ends igzo_tft_cv
*
* WHAT THIS WRAPPER DOES NOT FIX.  Level 1 derives its own gated-channel
* charge from Tox = 22.1 nm, an SiO2-EQUIVALENT thickness chosen to reproduce
* Cox, and it knows nothing about the semiconductor.  That part of the
* capacitance therefore still behaves as if the oxide were the whole stack.
*
* It cannot be corrected from outside: the semiconductor sits in SERIES with
* the oxide, and a series element cannot be added by hanging another capacitor
* in parallel - the attempt produces a negative capacitance, which is how this
* limitation was found rather than assumed.  Correcting it needs a model with
* a real surface potential, not level 1.
*
* So the estimate corrects the OVERLAP, which the wrapper owns explicitly, and
* leaves the channel term alone.  On the validated device (W = 1000 um,
* L = 8 um, ov = 5 um) the overlap is 7.8 pF per side against 12.5 pF of
* channel.  Simulated end to end, the total gate capacitance goes from
* 28.14 pF with the oxide alone to 24.25 pF with the semiconductor in
* series - a drop of 13.8 %.  Real, but not the whole answer: the channel
* term inside level 1 is untouched, so the true figure is larger than this.
